        "waitUntilStopped": [],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your eyes, so it is not fully a driving lesson. You will want your eyes, because the lesson points at something written down. You can listen to all of it now and come back to the parts that need looking at once you have stopped."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four recalls, at four distances."
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "RECALL",
              "instruction": "say the ending that makes an ordinal — R1, one lesson back",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU RECALL: say the ending that makes an ordinal — **R1**, one lesson back]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "RECALL",
              "instruction": "say Bhāratāt and name the ending that means \"from\" — R2, five lessons back",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU RECALL: say *Bhāratāt* and name the ending that means \"from\" — **R2**, five lessons back]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "RECALL",
              "instruction": "say iti — R3, twenty-two lessons back",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU RECALL: say *iti* — **R3**, twenty-two lessons back]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "RECALL",
              "instruction": "say prāyaḥ — R4, eighty lessons back",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU RECALL: say *prāyaḥ* — **R4**, eighty lessons back]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: सप्तमः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "सप्तमः (saptamaḥ) — \"seventh\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Build it before you read it: you have सप्त, and you have -म."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "सप्त + म. Nothing else changed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Chapter nineteen showed you that सप्त, Latin septem and English seven are the same word travelling three roads. The ordinal carries the same news one step further."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin's seventh is septimus. Look at the two endings side by side: Sanskrit -म, Latin -mus. That is not a coincidence and it is not a borrowing — it is one inherited ordinal ending, worn slightly differently in two mouths."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English went the other way and used a different suffix for all of them, which is why English says seven-th where Latin says septi-mus. Both endings are old. Sanskrit kept both, and the rest of this chapter and the next is the story of which numbers got which."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A second ordinal, made rather than learned, and the first sign that the ending is older than Sanskrit."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "sapta, then saptamaḥ",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *sapta*, then *saptamaḥ*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "pañcamaḥ, then saptamaḥ",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *pañcamaḥ*, then *saptamaḥ*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the Latin word with the same ending — septimus",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the Latin word with the same ending — *septimus*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is सप्त plus -म? (सप्तमः (saptamaḥ).) Which Latin word shows the same ending? (Septimus.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Source: Macdonell, A Practical Sanskrit Dictionary, p. 335 (DSAL)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
